        "\n",
        "## A first look at OpenFOAM\n",
        "\n",
        "From Tutorial 2 you will run turbulent channel-flow simulations in\n",
        "OpenFOAM. As a preview, turbulence modelling is configured in\n",
        "`constant/turbulenceProperties`:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{.cpp filename=\"constant/turbulenceProperties\"}\n",
        "/*--------------------------------*- C++ -*----------------------------------*\\\n",
        "| =========                 |                                                 |\n",
        "| \\\\      /  F ield         | OpenFOAM: The Open Source CFD Toolbox           |\n",
        "\\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/\n",
        "FoamFile\n",
        "{\n",
        "    version     2.0;\n",
        "    format      ascii;\n",
        "    class       dictionary;\n",
        "    object      turbulenceProperties;\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "simulationType  RAS;\n",
        "\n",
        "RAS\n",
        "{\n",
        "    RASModel        kOmegaSST;   // <1>\n",
        "    turbulence      on;\n",
        "    printCoeffs     on;          // <2>\n",
        "}\n",
        "```\n",
        "1. The turbulence model — we will compare `kOmegaSST` and `kEpsilon` later in the course.\n",
        "2. Prints the model coefficients to the log at startup, useful for reproducibility.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-important}\n",
        "## Task 2 — Wall treatment\n",
        "\n",
        "In one short paragraph: what near-wall mesh resolution (in terms of the\n",
        "first-cell $y^+$) does a *wall-resolved* RANS computation require, and how\n",
        "does this differ when *wall functions* are used? Relate your answer to the\n",
        "regions identified in Task 1.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.content-visible when-profile=\"solutions\"}\n",
        "::: {.solution-box}\n",
        "Wall-resolved: first cell centre at $y^+ \\lesssim 1$ (inside the viscous\n",
        "sublayer) with gradual growth. Wall functions: first cell placed in the\n",
        "log-law region, $30 \\lesssim y^+ \\lesssim 300$, since the wall function\n",
        "bridges the sublayer and buffer layer analytically. Placing the first cell\n",
        "in the buffer layer ($5$–$30$) is worst — neither approach is valid there.\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::\n",
